BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:knowcase-sonar-cotec-open-call-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T212305Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260907
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260908
SUMMARY:Deadline: KNOWCASE Open Call 2027: permanent digital-culture instal
 lation in Madrid (Sonar+D x Fundacion Cotec)
DESCRIPTION:Fundacion Cotec (a Spanish innovation-focused non-profit) and S
 onar+D\, building on an initial experiment at Sonar 2026\, invite proposal
 s for a long-term installation at KNOWCASE\, the street-level showcase of 
 Cotec's Madrid headquarters in Chamberi\, visible to the public 24 hours a
  day. THEME: digital culture\, meaning projects that combine knowledge\, r
 esearch\, science and data with art\, technology and social impact. Cotec'
 s stated priorities are the big transitions (analogue to digital\, linear 
 to circular economy\, tangible to intangible) plus innovation ecosystems a
 nd R&D&I\, the climate crisis\, the future of work and learning\, social d
 ivides\, citizen participation and democratic health. WHAT YOU GET: a budg
 et of up to EUR 100\,000 plus VAT covering everything (design\, production
 \, installation\, maintenance\, documentation\, artist fees\, travel\, acc
 ommodation and insurance)\, and a minimum 24-month public display from Feb
 ruary 2027. WHO CAN APPLY: artists\, collectives\, designers\, technologis
 ts\, universities\, research centres\, startups\, businesses\, creative ag
 encies\, producers\, cultural institutions and multidisciplinary teams\, i
 nternationally\; applicants must demonstrate solid experience and a releva
 nt portfolio. CONSTRAINTS: the proposal must be original and never previou
 sly exhibited\, must include participative or interactive elements\, must 
 encourage critical thinking\, and must be technically and financially viab
 le for the full 24+ month exhibition period (durability and maintenance ar
 e central selection criteria). Selection weighs conceptual quality highest
  (40 percent)\, followed by public interactivity (25 percent). HOW TO APPL
 Y: one PDF of maximum 15 pages containing summary\, concept\, technical pr
 oposal\, visuals\, budget\, timeline\, team profile\, portfolio and declar
 ation\, in Spanish\, another official language of Spain\, or English\, via
  the application portal linked from the terms and conditions. Results anno
 unced 14 October 2026. Deadline 7 September 2026 at midnight CEST.\n\nOrga
 nisation: Fundacion Cotec and Sonar+D\nLocation: Installation at the KNOWC
 ASE storefront of Fundacion Cotec's headquarters in Chamberi\, Madrid\, Sp
 ain\, a street-level venue visible 24/7. The call is international\; appli
 cations accepted in Spanish\, other official languages of Spain\, or Engli
 sh.\nAward: Up to EUR 100\,000 (plus VAT)\, all-inclusive: conceptualisati
 on\, design\, technical development\, production\, installation\, maintena
 nce\, documentation\, artist fees\, travel\, accommodation and insurance.\
 nDuration: Installation displayed for a minimum of 24 months\, starting Fe
 bruary 2027\n\nMore: https://sonar.es/en/news/knowcase-open-call-2027?utm_
 source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://sonar.es/en/news/knowcase-open-call-2027?utm_source=artificialn
 ouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Installation at the KNOWCASE storefront of Fundacion Cotec's headq
 uarters in Chamberi\, Madrid\, Spain\, a street-level venue visible 24/7. 
 The call is international\; applications accepted in Spanish\, other offic
 ial languages of Spain\, or English.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:project-liberty-co-lab-fund-round-2-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T212305Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260729
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260730
SUMMARY:Deadline: Project Liberty: Co-Lab Fund Micro-Grants (Round 2\, 2026
 )
DESCRIPTION:MEMBERS ONLY: this is a member-led collaboration fund\, not an 
 open public call. Projects must be led by a Project Liberty Coalition (All
 iance) member organization. Round 2 shifts to micro-grants of USD 10\,000 
 or less\, an iterative change from Round 1 intended to spread support acro
 ss more of the Coalition\; organizations that did not receive a Round 1 aw
 ard are preferred. SCOPE: micro-grants fund two kinds of work\, (1) discre
 te projects that strengthen the Coalition itself\, such as Shared Services
  or convenings\, and (2) time-sensitive collaborative projects within a de
 signated workstream. In both cases the work should begin quickly and produ
 ce something concrete. A STRONG APPLICATION SHOWS: work that builds the Co
 alition as an entity\, such as shared tools\, infrastructure or connection
 s that make joint action easier\; clear alignment with a Coalition workstr
 eam\; value for multiple Coalition organizations\; a strong case that the 
 work would not happen\, or would move more slowly\, without the Fund\; a c
 oncrete outcome such as a tool\, resource\, convening\, campaign or resear
 ch product\; and readiness to begin substantive work within two weeks of a
 pproval. LIMIT: one application per organization\, submitted through the e
 xisting Co-Lab Fund application form. Round 1 results were still pending a
 t the time of this call\, with funded projects to be shared soon and engag
 ement opportunities expected across the work. Prospective applicants unsur
 e whether an idea fits this round are invited to reach out first. DEADLINE
 : 29 July 2026 at midnight. Background on the Co-Lab program is in the Coa
 lition's overview document.\n\nOrganisation: Project Liberty (Alliance Ste
 erCo)\; pooled fund supported by Future of Life Institute\, Omidyar Networ
 k\, Ergo Impact\, and Project Liberty\nLocation: Members of the Project Li
 berty coalition (Alliance). Projects led by a Coalition member organizatio
 n\; geography otherwise unrestricted.\nAward: Micro-grants of USD 10\,000 
 or less\, a deliberate change from Round 1's larger awards so the Fund rea
 ches more of the Coalition. Preference given to organizations that did not
  receive a Round 1 award. No application fee.\nDuration: Short\, discrete 
 projects. Applicants must be ready to begin substantive work within two we
 eks of approval. This is the final round of the summer\; the Fund is expec
 ted to resume later in 2026.\n\nMore: https://forms.fillout.com/t/6AGEGhTk
 NJus?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://forms.fillout.com/t/6AGEGhTkNJus?utm_source=artificialnouveaugr
 antdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Members of the Project Liberty coalition (Alliance). Projects led 
 by a Coalition member organization\; geography otherwise unrestricted.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:scaling-ai-safety-multi-agent-world-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T212305Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260808
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260809
SUMMARY:Deadline: Scaling AI Safety for a Multi-Agent World (Joint Funding 
 Call)
DESCRIPTION:A joint philanthropic funding call by Schmidt Sciences\, Google
  DeepMind\, ARIA\, the Cooperative AI Foundation and Google.org to catalys
 e the foundational scientific research needed to understand\, evaluate and
  control risks emerging from large-scale ecosystems of interacting AI agen
 ts deployed by multiple actors (multi-principal\, multi-agent settings). T
 he call argues that focusing only on the safety and alignment of individua
 l models is insufficient\, and seeks system-level approaches. RESEARCH AGE
 NDA (four clusters): (1) Sandboxes and Testbeds\, realistic\, reproducible
  multi-agent environments for studying populations of frontier-model agent
 s\; (2) The Science of Agent Networks\, how collective capabilities emerge
  and scale\, how networks fail\, and how dangerous population-level proper
 ties can be detected\; (3) Strengthening Agent Infrastructure\, evaluating
  and stress-testing primitives such as identity\, verifiability\, reputati
 on\, communication and commitment\; (4) Multi-Agent Oversight and Control\
 , detection\, attribution\, security and intervention methods to keep depl
 oyed agent populations safe at scale. Proposals may target one cluster or 
 span several\, with priority on depth over breadth and on realistic sandbo
 xes and testbeds. OUT OF SCOPE: single-agent safety\, capability advanceme
 nt without a clear safety motivation\, toy systems\, naive application of 
 pre-existing solutions\, commercial product development\, and purely conce
 ptual/policy work. ELIGIBILITY: individual researchers\, research teams\, 
 research institutions and multi-institution collaborations across universi
 ties\, national laboratories\, institutes and nonprofit research organisat
 ions\; open globally. Informational webinars on 30 June and 23 July 2026\;
  notification of decisions in autumn 2026. Proposals due 8 August 2026 by 
 11:59pm Anywhere on Earth. Contact: multiagentsafety@schmidtsciences.org. 
 Apply at https://schmidtsciences.smapply.io/prog/scaling_ai_safety_for_a_m
 ulti_agent_world/.\n\nOrganisation: Schmidt Sciences\, Google DeepMind\, t
 he Advanced Research & Invention Agency (ARIA)\, the Cooperative AI Founda
 tion\, and Google.org\nLocation: Global\; open worldwide\, with collaborat
 ions across geographic boundaries encouraged.\nAward: Two funding tiers (a
 pplicants may apply to either or both)\, projects 1 to 2 years. Tier 1: up
  to USD 300\,000 for exploratory research\, pilot studies\, or focused tec
 hnical investigations. Tier 2: USD 300\,000 to USD 1\,000\,000 for more am
 bitious or collaborative projects. For projects funded by Schmidt Sciences
 \, indirect costs must be at or below 10%. No application fee.\nDuration: 
 1 to 2 years\n\nMore: https://schmidtsciences.smapply.io/prog/scaling_ai_s
 afety_for_a_multi_agent_world/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_m
 edium=referral
URL:https://schmidtsciences.smapply.io/prog/scaling_ai_safety_for_a_multi_a
 gent_world/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Global\; open worldwide\, with collaborations across geographic bo
 undaries encouraged.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:spencer-foundation-vision-grants-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T212305Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260812
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260813
SUMMARY:Deadline: Spencer Foundation: Vision Grants 2026 (Research Planning
  Grants)
DESCRIPTION:Vision Grants are research planning grants that give scholars a
 nd their collaborators the time\, space\, resources and support to plan a 
 large-scale study or program of research focused on transforming education
  systems toward greater equity. Rather than a fully fleshed-out research p
 lan\, the proposal is an invitation to think forward about what research i
 s needed to transform education systems toward equity and how that systems
  change will happen\, identifying the system(s) targeted and the specific 
 levers the team thinks must be engaged. Grants bring together a team for 1
 2 to 18 months to collaboratively develop ambitious\, large-scale\, cross-
 disciplinary research projects co-designed with practitioners\, policymake
 rs\, communities and other partners. Awarded teams also join a cohort lear
 ning program held in person in Chicago. ELIGIBILITY: PIs and Co-PIs must h
 ave appropriate experience or an earned doctorate in an academic disciplin
 e or a terminal degree in a professional field\; graduate students may be 
 on the team but cannot be PI or Co-PI. The PI must be affiliated with a no
 n-profit or public/governmental institution willing to serve as the admini
 stering organization (colleges\, universities\, school districts\, researc
 h facilities\, or other 501(c)(3) non-profits\, or non-US equivalents)\; t
 he Spencer Foundation does not award grants directly to individuals. Open 
 to applicants in the US and internationally\; proposals in English\, budge
 ts in USD. PIs and Co-PIs may apply even with another active Spencer grant
  or proposal in review\, but may not be part of more than one Vision Grant
  proposal. Note: a Vision Grant is a prerequisite for applying to Spencer'
 s Transformative Research Grant program (TRG\, USD 3.5 million)\, though r
 eceiving one does not guarantee a TRG. PROCESS: applications opened 4 June
  2026. A required Intent to Apply form (max 200 words\, non-binding) is du
 e 12 August 2026 at 12:00 PM noon Central time\; the Full Proposal (2000-w
 ord narrative) is due 16 September 2026 at 12:00 PM noon Central time. Pro
 gram contact: Jasmine Knetl\, visiongrants@spencer.org.\n\nOrganisation: S
 pencer Foundation\nLocation: Worldwide\; PI must be affiliated with a non-
 profit or public/governmental institution (US 501(c)(3) or non-US equivale
 nt) that will serve as the administering organization. Proposals accepted 
 from the US and internationally\; English only\; budgets in US Dollars.\nA
 ward: USD 75\,000 total per grant\; no indirect cost charges permitted. No
  application fee.\nDuration: 12 to 18 months (research planning grant)\n\n
 More: https://www.spencer.org/grant_types/vision-grants?utm_source=artific
 ialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.spencer.org/grant_types/vision-grants?utm_source=artificial
 nouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Worldwide\; PI must be affiliated with a non-profit or public/gove
 rnmental institution (US 501(c)(3) or non-US equivalent) that will serve a
 s the administering organization. Proposals accepted from the US and inter
 nationally\; English only\; budgets in US Dollars.
END:VEVENT
